{Kevin J. O`Connell, James B. Adams} 
1.e4 c5 2.Nc3 Nc6 3.g3 g6 4.Bg2 Bg7 {[%cal Yg7d4,Yc5d4,Yc6d4]} 5.d3 {After
White's fifth move Black must take counter-measures against the intended 
sequence of Le3, Dd2, Sd1, c3, Se2 and, at least d4.} 5...d6 6.Nge2 {
Spassky's successful system was 6.f4 followed by 7.Sf3 and 8.0-0, a line 
which brought Geller down three times in the candidats match - all to 
K-side assaults in which the white pieces and pawns cooperated well in 
storming the black fortress.} 6...Qd7 $6 {Black has in mind a very 
interesting mode of development.} 7.Be3 b6 8.f4 Bb7 {Now the idea is clear
- Black intends to castle long to sidestep the fury of a K-side offensive.
} 9.O-O Nd4 $6 10.Qd2 h5 11.h3 f5 12.Bf2 $16 O-O-O 13.Nxd4 $1 Bxd4 14.Bxd4
cxd4 15.Ne2 e5 16.c3 $1 {Now Black has real problems.} 16...h4 17.cxd4 
hxg3 18.dxe5 $18 Ne7 19.Qc3+ Kb8 20.Nxg3 fxe4 21.dxe4 d5 22.Rad1 d4 23.Qd2
Rxh3 $2 24.Bxh3 Qxh3 25.Qg2 Qh4 26.Rf3 $1 Rh8 27.Rxd4 Nf5 28.exf5 gxf5 29.
Rdd3 Rg8 30.Qh2 1-0

